#edb/ref 17592186334027} {:answer/id #uuid "c360afef-02b3-5232-b951-ae761d89057d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334684} {:answer/id #uuid "fc79cebb-683f-5ec6-b279-a40dd479a779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e4ea13-feef-5acf-9b9a-0e579d2765d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334027} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a1eb48a-b984-5dff-bbdc-6a2962f92e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0efeeff3-47fe-5165-905e-7ef041b1d387" :question/math-academy-id "q-65073" :question/problem "![](images/96/96b22799c75a4b19df4c6bddf8b63d11a7d0cab8a0cc481b8466a29eb1a2af15.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be5fee5-ff9c-5713-906a-6d90f5a9cb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{54}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256895} {:answer/id #uuid "15efda3b-3678-57d9-8d54-1db2e510c65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256978} {:answer/id #uuid "26ace001-a008-507d-a0e9-16b26952290c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257114} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0cbc61-b93d-54e4-b926-1de5e2999404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257176} {:answer/id #uuid "60c8fc36-e289-5c4e-a0d0-f7e4ddae3e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c8fc36-e289-5c4e-a0d0-f7e4ddae3e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257633} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a8e351-07c7-5816-954b-d7240ccbef42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adb42fa3-fa6c-5e9a-800e-ffdd8192d291" :question/math-academy-id "q-124789" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e2/e2323a87ec4ab790714f4f6f5e5ff359495e93c6be5d76ae4b9f7ff15d14fd90.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{14}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14\\cdot 11}}{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148d9fa6-e709-5ed3-8dee-a9794d49d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256967} {:answer/id #uuid "57004998-41ca-5e4e-90f5-2c24561a08c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee702e6-7a9d-5c39-9f0f-93b8b0fe4698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913} {:answer/id #uuid "d244da59-6698-5edd-a3a0-8e822fc76895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258615} {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654efa3-cd29-5e20-bbaf-04bd03e93e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba2d6f5-ab4c-55d2-aafd-8aaa2c0dbeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030abc43-15ab-542a-9efc-a5b8959e8901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256806} {:answer/id #uuid "164f1f56-0609-51b8-a92f-5f6f8b2829eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256984} {:answer/id #uuid "b606a516-6e71-50b3-b37f-00403c1f1485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258363} {:answer/id #uuid "bae703ab-5b1b-5842-8663-291050f98b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258418} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d72a9c-545b-5d53-89de-2aadd8702aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3d72a9c-545b-5d53-89de-2aadd8702aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79ad56d-ed57-5258-b07c-74c9f1f477d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9926f7c6-9c2e-5943-b7e8-596de1b982b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124796" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee92f92a52d8ade2052a62a5c19906cd86027b47f22ec986a740b9379e66d8d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{8})}=3\\sqrt{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} {:answer/id #uuid "a034f5c0-3b14-5e29-8def-edc95efbd80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334397} {:answer/id #uuid "cb74b243-d73b-5089-bae4-f981f0a60cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334760} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ffa90f-9b1f-53a0-9ae4-72f9fe2ebb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334836} {:answer/id #uuid "d8504233-ad25-5121-8f69-909d3fab6b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b238c5f-814a-5a46-a972-3a25a21b520c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8585ed26-d09c-5c8e-84bf-c2d2c03840ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-64730" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194eaee8-54cb-563d-8f6d-d34f552da442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333221} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcc27a4-b6ff-5196-bfc9-478bbd73a589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334253} {:answer/id #uuid "a96bc338-b51a-5af0-adad-0e96975b2300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334473} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} {:answer/id #uuid "e6417934-5eda-53e4-82d9-b2e434d37de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfce3d7a-f48e-534b-a3f3-76cd7412d6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7581029a-054b-5d43-92f5-a2b561001b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65072" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fc/fc291f1fc5c4a712d3eef7ea2872de621855669d97445b24a2bc7a9da55949d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b84d4ec-9f5f-5a47-8517-3bb8f8c9e6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333094} {:answer/id #uuid "0f97daea-fc44-5b9a-b6c8-8ae1ec14226d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333127} {:answer/id #uuid "26887548-0fc3-51f2-9193-efc1f3262179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333343} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0d2d95-0c48-5abf-8954-df6ad2d3263b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334390} {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db